=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The dc_clos_bgp fabric is fully healthy at the time of this investigation. All five BGP sessions in the spine/super-spine layer are established and stable, with correct eBGP route propagation: both host prefixes (10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24) are present in the RIB of every router along every forwarding path. End-to-end ICMP is confirmed bidirectionally between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) at both small and maximum-MTU (1500-byte) frame sizes — zero packet loss in all tests. Traceroute confirms the correct 3-hop forwarding path through leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 with no blackholing or asymmetry. No iptables rules, no tc rate-limiters, no interface errors, and IP forwarding is active on all transit routers.

Two observations were investigated and ruled out as faults: (1) spine_router_0_0 receives zero prefixes from super_spine_router_0 — this is expected eBGP AS-path loop prevention behaviour (the super-spine reflects routes that already contain AS 65100 back to spine_router_0_0, which correctly rejects them); (2) all leaf-to-leaf traffic currently traverses only spine_router_0_0 with no ECMP to spine_router_0_1 — this is an artefact of BGP best-path selection preferring the peer with the lower router-ID (172.16.0.1 over 172.16.0.3) and is a design-level single-path concern, not an active failure. The most likely explanation for the helpdesk ticket is a transient blackhole during the initial BGP convergence window (log timestamps show the fabric came up just minutes before investigation). No corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
=== END ===